  "notes": "The Pacific-Northwest post-grunge producer whose room sound became 2000s radio rock, Gate-2 clear at 1991. 77–164 with the flagship singles at 90–133 straight; the down-tuned riff songs \"feel **half-time** at ~90\", which is the other backbeat class and is named here rather than encoded. The research restricts this entry to the 2001–2002 Vancouver-era productions because the producer-side lineage otherwise overlaps the vol. 1 Joey Moi entry, and that boundary is respected here. The defining fact is a room: \"live kit in a real room, tracked big and compressed\", with a documented regional chain — tracked in Burnaby, mixed in Vancouver, mastered at Sterling. A note generator hears a real room as a played kit, so this is where `humanize` opens: a real velocity spread and per-lane jitter, which is exactly what separates this model from the Nickelback artist entry that shares the record and is gridded hard. Three deltas are quoted: \"kick on 1 and the '&' of 2 (8th double-kick under choruses)\", \"gated snare on 2&4\", and \"crash on every chorus downbeat\" with \"fills are 16th tom runs into a crash\". Harmony is flat-side minors and drop-tuned major riffs \"in power-chord voicings with no thirds, so the vocal decides major/minor\" — the high triad share and the open voicing. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium and says the drum specifics are partly convention-derived. ⚠ Every drum probability here is **INFERRED**, as is `bpm.mode` 110.",
